{"study_accession":"PRJNA636503","secondary_study_accession":"SRP265526","title":"Phase I Dose Escalation Study of ATRA Combined with the LSD1 inhibitor Tranylcypromine in AML and MDS","description":"Preclinical studies have shown that combining the LSD1 inhibitor tranylcypromine (TCP) with all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) induces differentiation and impairs survival in non-APL acute myeloid leukemia (AML). We conducted a Phase 1 clinical trial (NCT02273102) to evaluate the safety and preliminary activity of ATRA in combination with TCP in patients with relapsed/refractory AML and myelodysplasia (MDS). Seventeen patients (11 AML and 6 MDS) received ATRA-TCP therapy with ATRA (45 mg/m2 daily in divided doses) and TCP (3 dose levels: 10 mg twice-daily [BID], 20 mg BID, and 30 mg BID). ATRA-TCP had an acceptable safety profile. The maximum tolerated dose of TCP was 20 mg BID. There were 3 DLTs: dizziness (20 mg BID), asthenia (30 mg BID), and nausea/vomiting (30 mg BID). Best evaluable responses included 1 morphologic leukemia-free state (MLFS), 1 marrow complete remission (CR) with hematologic improvement, 2 stable disease with hematologic improvement, and 2 stable disease. In 13 response-evaluable patients, the overall response rate was 30.8% and clinical benefit rate 46.2%. Gene expression profiling of patient blasts showed that responding patients had a more dormant phenotype compared to non-responders at baseline. In human AML cell lines, we showed that treatment with ATRA-TCP increases differentiation capacity and/or cell death, and that ATRA-TCP regulates the in vitro expression of genes that segregate primary patients by their clinical response. These data indicate that LSD1 inhibition sensitizes AML cells to ATRA-induced differentiation and cell death and may restore clinical responsiveness in subsets of MDS and AML patients. Overall design: Total RNA-seq of purified CD34+ cell isolated from bone marrow of Relapsed/refractory AML and MDS patients before/after treatment with ATRA-TCP (phase-I clinical trial, NCT02273102). Total RNA-seq of ATRA-treated, TCP-treated, and ATRA-TCP treated or vehicle control in U-937 and SKNO-1 cell lines in duplicates.","pubmed":"33495312","geo_series":"","n_samples":14,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN15077676","study_accession":"PRJNA636503","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"RNA_p18_Baseline, AML","description":"RNA_p18_Baseline, AML","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM4586408","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2021-06-24","attributes":[["source_name","CD34+ leukemic blasts"],["disease","AML"],["tissue","bone marrow"],["cell source","Cryopreserved patient sample"],["treatment","Baseline"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR11905180","experiment_accession":"SRX8451801","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":78388430,"base_count":15834462860,"first_public":"2021-06-24","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: GSM4586408: RNA_p18_Baseline Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN15077677","study_accession":"PRJNA636503","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"RNA_p17_Baseline, AML","description":"RNA_p17_Baseline, AML","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM4586407","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2021-06-24","attributes":[["source_name","CD34+ leukemic blasts"],["disease","AML"],["tissue","bone marrow"],["cell source","Cryopreserved patient sample"],["treatment","Baseline"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR11905179","experiment_accession":"SRX8451800","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":65587470,"base_count":13248668940,"first_public":"2021-06-24","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: GSM4586407: RNA_p17_Baseline Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN15077678","study_accession":"PRJN